#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068429 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "aa51996a-09e5-5950-8438-795ab3e0f027" :question/math-academy-id "e-10008" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 2x + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{1}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{blue}{1}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  + 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068430 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/id #uuid "06ed73b3-1442-5b21-a317-961d0395f7da" :question/math-academy-id "e-10009" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 12x + 40.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{6}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{40}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{blue}{6}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  +4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068672 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"}] :question/id #uuid "2acac780-2b17-5489-accc-2a3a090784e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9927" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 + 14x + 49) - 8$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+14x+49 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+14x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 7x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+14x+49)-8 & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2}-8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068677 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/id #uuid "b107ed10-4501-50ad-8ad7-231fd933bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9997" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 - 12x + 36) -5$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+36 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-12x+36)-5 & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2}-5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d53fe67-7a37-5135-9bda-59dc26da7eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400939} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c7488-0854-5209-9e72-78867a6a1be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400940} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ec775e-a603-50a4-a821-b02cc8bdf4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-10,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400941} {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400942} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e81c67-49e3-5deb-9f3d-4bfdd5f4f433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400942} :answer-field/id #uuid "c559c184-d951-52c2-ae9b-1fd6b5cd4853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a81398bc-73ed-504a-b8b3-ef512650d7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141622" :question/problem "If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c13619bc-5da4-58c3-8392-a51654138df3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066329} {:answer-field/id #uuid "64193fcd-9f32-5d02-803f-4dc7d03003ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066330}] :question/id #uuid "1b8e89c5-8930-56e1-94ae-d8d80995aed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-250390" :question/problem "If the expression $x^{2} + 14x + 31$ is written as $(x + p)^{2} + q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q$?$p =$\n{{field-1}},\n$q =$\n{{field-2}}"}]}
